 exercises, net of shares withheld  |  |  |  |  |  | 1   |  |  | —   |  |  | —   |  |  | —   |  |  | —   |  |  | —   |\nL611: Issuance of common stock in connection with Employee Share Purchase Plan  |  |  |  |  |  | —   |  |  | —   |  |  | —   |  |  | —   |  |  | —   |  |  | —   |\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nhood-20260930 (https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1783879/000178387926000167/hood-20260930.htm)\nciteturn22view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn16view0\",\"lineno\":1418}); Total lines: 4228\nL1415: Our exposure to losses in the Trust is limited to the carrying value of net assets held by the Trust, including expected credit losses related to the purchased credit card receivables (Refer to Note 7 - Allowance for Credit Losses). For the Trust, the creditors have no recourse to our general credit and the liabilities of the Trust can only be settled by the Trust’s assets. Additionally, the assets of the Trust can only be used to settle obligations of the Trust.\nL1416: As of September 30, 2026, the Trust maintained six revolving borrowing arrangements (with Barclays, SVB, WF, Truist Bank, Goldman Sachs, and Mizuho) providing an aggregate commitment limit of $1.55 billion. These facilities have revolving periods ending between April 2027 and November 2029. Borrowings generally bear interest at a commercial paper rate, Term SOFR, or Daily Simple SOFR plus applicable margins ranging from 1.20% to 1.50%. Undrawn amounts accrue commitment fees between 0.20% and 0.35%.\nL1417: In October 2026, the Trust amended its revolving borrowing arrangement with Barclays to increase the commitment limit by $100 million and reduce the applicable margin by 0.20%. As of September 30, 2026, the weighted-average interest rate of the active arrangements was 5.06%.\nL1418: For the year ended December 31, 2025 and during the nine months ended September 30, 2026, the Trust purchased $5.2 billion and $10.8 billion of credit card receivables. As of December 31, 2025 and September 30, 2026, the carrying value of purchased credit card receivables that had not been collected, net of provision for credit losses, was $786 million and $1.51 billion, and the outstanding balance of borrowing principal and interest was $602 million and $839 million.\nL1419: For the three and nine months ended September 30, 2025, the net interest revenue of the Trust was $14 million and $26 million. For the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026, the net interest revenue of the Trust was $46 million and $107 million.\nL1420: On July 23, 2026, the Trust issued $500 million aggregate principal amount of Series 2026-1 notes with a weighted average coupon of approximately 4.86%. The Series 2026-1 notes are comprised of $422 million principal amount of 4.80% notes, $48 million principal amount of 4.99% notes, $20 million principal amount of 5.31% notes, and $10 million principal amount of 5.65% notes, all of which have a revolving period ending in June 2029 and a final maturity date in July 2031.\nL1421: We received net cash proceeds of $497 million, in connection with the issuance of the Series 2026-1 notes, which were used primarily to repay outstanding borrowings under the Trust’s existing revolving\nL1422: \nL1423: 41\nL1424: \nL1425: * * *\nL1426: \nL1427: 【35†Table of Contents】\nL1428: warehouse facilities. The Series 2026-1 notes are carried at amortized cost, adjusted for the fair value hedging adjustment described in Note 12 - Derivatives and Hedging Activities. Debt issuance costs of approximately $2.54 million are presented as a direct deduction from the carrying amount of the Series 2026-1 notes and are amortized to interest expense over the contractual term of the Series 2026-1 notes.\nL1429: As of September 30, 2026, the outstanding principal amount was $500 million, the net carrying amount was $488 million, and the remaining unamortized debt issuance costs were $2.44 million.\nL1430: The indenture governing the Series 2026-1 notes contains a provision that allows the Trust to redeem the notes in full at any time after June 1, 2029, at the full principal amount plus accrued and unpaid interest and fees. The indenture also contains early amortization events, including a servicer default, certain insolvency events, and the three-month average excess spread percentage falling to 0.00% or below.\nL1431: If an early amortization event occurs, the revolving period ends and principal collections on the receivables are used to repay the notes on each monthly payment date until the notes are paid in full. Interest on the notes is payable monthly in arrears.\nL1432: On October 15, 2026, the Trust completed an issuance of $500 million of Series 2026-2 notes, which are secured primarily by a pool of credit card receivables originated by Coastal Bank and part of the Trust collateral. The offering consisted of four classes of fixed-rate notes that were issued to qualified institutional buyers in a private offering pursuant to Rule 144A and Regulation S under the Securities Act.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRobinhood 2025 10-K/A (https://investors.robinhood.com/static-files/36353485-5cd1-4eb2-9eb4-6d266f75374b)\nciteturn22view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 203; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn11view0\",\"lineno\":5780}); Total lines: 8655\nL5769@P131: assurance regarding the reliability of financial reporting and the preparation of financial statements\nL5770@P131: for external purposes in accordance with generally accepted accounting principles. A company’s\nL5771@P131: internal control over financial reporting includes those policies and procedures that (1) pertain to the\nL5772@P131: maintenance of records that, in reasonable detail, accurately and fairly reflect the transactions and\nL5773@P131: dispositions of the assets of the company; (2) provide reasonable assurance that transactions are\nL5774@P131: recorded as necessary to permit preparation of financial statements in accordance with generally\nL5775@P131: accepted accounting principles, and that receipts and expenditures of the company are being made\nL5776@P131: only in accordance with authorizations of management and directors of the company; and (3) provide\nL5777@P131: reasonable assurance regarding prevention or timely detection of unauthorized acquisition, use, or\nL5778@P131: disposition of the company’s assets that could have a material effect on the financial statements.\nL5779@P131: Because of its inherent limitations, internal control over financial reporting may not prevent or detect\nL5780@P131: misstatements. Also, projections of any evaluation of effectiveness to future periods are subject to\nL5781@P131: the risk that controls may become inadequate because of changes in conditions, or that the degree\nL5782@P131: of compliance with the policies or procedures may deteriorate.\nL5783@P131: /s/ Ernst & Young LLP\nL5784@P131: San Francisco, California\nL5785@P131: February 18, 2026\nL5786@P131: 128\nL5787@P131-132: Table of ContentsDecember 31,\nL5788@P132: (in millions, except share and per share data) 2024 2025\nL5789@P132: Assets\nL5790@P132: Current assets:\nL5791@P132: Cash and cash equivalents ................................................................................................................................................. $ 4,332 $ 4,261\nL5792@P132: Cash, cash equivalents, and securities segregated under federal and other regulations......................................... 4,724 5,749\nL5793@P132: Receivables from brokers, dealers, and clearing organizations.................................................................................... 471 426\nL5794@P132: Receivables from users, net................................................................................................